# A destructive result and a sibling operand of its instruction, both in the frame.
#
# `add` writes its result over operand zero, so legalization copies operand zero into the result's
# location in front of the instruction. Any *other* operand sitting in that same location is read
# after that copy has already overwritten it - which is why the placer keeps such a pair apart, and
# why it cannot do so by comparing their lives: a tied result's interval begins exactly where the
# operand it is written over ends, so the two never overlap and every interference test in the
# allocator sees them as mergeable.
#
# Placement states that separately, as `tieConflicts`, and asks it wherever a location is handed
# out. It used to ask in one of the two places - a register - and the frame recycled a slot on
# interval overlap alone, so a pair that had been refused a shared register was handed a shared slot
# instead. `%y` and `%r` below are that pair.
#
# Everything here is arranged so that both of them end up in the frame and so that `%y`'s slot is the
# lowest-numbered one free where `%r` asks for one:
#
#   - `%v0`..`%v11` are live from their definitions to the very end, which holds every register for
#     the whole function and leaves nothing for anything defined after them;
#   - `%y` is a `mul`, whose fixed rax/rdx shape denies it the registers a longer-lived value left,
#     so it is the first web to go to the frame and takes slot 0;
#   - `%p0` and `%p1` are defined after it and are live across the `add`, so they hold the slots
#     above `%y`'s and nothing below it is occupied;
#   - `%r` is live from the `add` to the last instruction, so it goes to the frame too, and the only
#     free slot when it asks is the one `%y` has just left.
#
# Without the tie check on the frame, `%r` takes `%y`'s slot and `verifyPlacement` reports `add: y is
# read from stack:N, which its result is written over`.
tiedResultAndSibling(%a: I32): I32 {
    {
        %v0 = add %a, 2: I32
        %v1 = add %a, 3: I32
        %v2 = add %a, 4: I32
        %v3 = add %a, 5: I32
        %v4 = add %a, 6: I32
        %v5 = add %a, 7: I32
        %v6 = add %a, 8: I32
        %v7 = add %a, 9: I32
        %v8 = add %a, 10: I32
        %v9 = add %a, 11: I32
        %v10 = add %a, 12: I32
        %v11 = add %a, 13: I32

        %y = mul %a, 101: I32
        %p0 = add %a, 40: I32
        %p1 = add %a, 41: I32

        # %y dies here and %r begins here, which is what makes their lives look shareable.
        %r = add %v0, %y: I32

        %c0 = add %a, %v0: I32
        %c1 = add %c0, %v1: I32
        %c2 = add %c1, %v2: I32
        %c3 = add %c2, %v3: I32
        %c4 = add %c3, %v4: I32
        %c5 = add %c4, %v5: I32
        %c6 = add %c5, %v6: I32
        %c7 = add %c6, %v7: I32
        %c8 = add %c7, %v8: I32
        %c9 = add %c8, %v9: I32
        %c10 = add %c9, %v10: I32
        %c11 = add %c10, %v11: I32
        %c12 = add %c11, %p0: I32
        %c13 = add %c12, %p1: I32
        %c14 = add %c13, %r: I32
        ret %c14
    }
}
